            <ArticleTitle>Conventional 22- and 20-gauge needle for second trimester amniocentesis: A comparison of short term outcomes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>27-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4274/tjod.74875</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the short-term outcomes of two different-sized needles for genetic amniocentesis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 271 amniocentesis were retrospectively evaluated in 2 groups concerning the size of the needles used during the procedure: Conventional 20-gauge (G) (n=164) and 22G (n=107). Periprocedural complications and cost-effectiveness were compared across the groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were no differences between groups concerning complications within 15 days after the procedure (fetal loss, 0.6% versus 0.9%, and amniotic fluid leak 1.2% versus 1.8%, p=0.99 for each). The 22G needle was significantly more cost efficient (p&lt;0.0001).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The 22 G spinal needle is convenient for second trimester amniocentesis with similar complication rate and has a favorable cost profile.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
